### Duration, unknown responses and text

Raw duration min/median/max are ${duration_min}/${duration_median}/${duration_max}.
Magnitude and minute alignment support an inferred seconds interpretation, producing
median ${duration_hours_median} hours and maximum ${duration_days_max} days. This is
an inference, not confirmed source metadata. Store hours/days for interpretation and
use log-transformed hours for modelling. Day-duration skewness is ${duration_skew};
log-hours skewness is ${duration_log_skew}.

${unknown_table}

Missing red-flag/comorbidity responses mean unknown. Explicit sentinels mean no
reported items. Both receive explicit indicators; real items are fixed-vocabulary
multi-hot features, unknown counts remain missing and explicit-none counts are zero.
Token normalization changes strings without claiming the original entries were all
incorrect. The table distinguishes raw and labelled denominators.

${text_missing_table}

The final structured model excludes these free-text fields. Optional description
TF-IDF exists behind a flag but is disabled in the saved model and current experiment
session; free-text symptom mining has not been completed. Severity and pain use
ordered codes, blood pressures remain separate numeric values, and each vital has a
missingness indicator. Repeat-request counts use only strictly earlier requests,
including unlabelled intake history; simultaneous/future requests do not contribute.

### Protocol heterogeneity and provenance

The assessment profile records ${protocol_n} distinct protocol labels:

${protocol_table}

Mixed adapted protocols, a local protocol and clinical judgment could encode
inconsistent thresholds or documentation practices. No repeated-rating agreement
study, protocol-specific adjudication or noise rate has been produced. Consequently,
protocol-related label noise is a plausible limitation rather than a measured
error rate. `triage_protocol` and every assessment field except the target are
excluded from predictors. A model that reproduces these labels may also reproduce
their inconsistencies.

Treat synthetic or limited case-study data as insufficient evidence of clinical
representativeness. The supplied artifacts do not independently establish whether
records are synthetic, how they were sampled, or whether protocol names correspond
to independently applied clinical procedures. Confirm provenance before reuse.

## Exploratory findings

The [EDA summary](@/artifacts/eda/summary.json) and
[interactive figure index](@/artifacts/eda/index.html) describe the entire labelled
cohort before model splitting. Findings nominate relationships for testing; they
are not independent confirmation of predictive or clinical value.

${eda_medians}

Age medians rise modestly, symptom duration medians fall, heart rate rises and oxygen
saturation falls across recorded urgency. The groups overlap; medians do not define
classification rules. Ages span ${age_min}–${age_max} years; ${younger_n} requests
are below the artifact's adult-age cutoff, so the cohort does not cover all ages.

${eda_category_table}

Severity and pain show pronounced differences in urgency composition. Recorded sex
shows similar composition, while pregnancy-response groups differ in both
applicability and sample makeup. Pregnancy associations should not be read as an
isolated causal effect. Sudden onset has a higher High/Critical share than gradual
onset in this cohort.

${eda_complaints}

Chief complaint patterns support investigation of respiratory and chest complaints,
with confounding by severity, pain and selected red flags still possible. The
multi-value plots show overlapping item frequencies rather than mutually exclusive
patient groups. Comorbidities have a smaller overall association than symptom
severity and red flags in the formal tests below.

${eda_correlations}

These are pairwise-complete Spearman correlations, with no imputation for EDA.
Correlations are descriptive, including the ordinal coding of urgency. Coupled blood
pressure missingness and missing vital/text data require care when interpreting
availability as a clinical signal.

A notable surprise is complete grouped-outcome separation: all ${separated_n}
requests reporting **${separated_item}** are High or Critical
(${separated_classes}). This warrants label-construction review; it does not establish
independent clinical validity. There are ${repeat_requests_n} labelled requests
beyond the first per patient and ${repeat_patients_n} patients with repeated labelled
requests, so treating all rows as independent would be inappropriate.

## Hypothesis testing and statistical interpretation

The saved workflow tests ${primary_n} primary comparisons and ${secondary_n}
individual-item secondary comparisons at alpha ${alpha}. Null/alternative statements,
assumptions, exclusion counts, group summaries, statistics and interpretation are in
[the omnibus CSV](@/artifacts/hypothesis_test_results.csv). The numeric null concerns
rank/distribution equality; categorical nulls concern independence. No causal claim
follows rejection.

Assumption procedure from the artifact: ${assumption_method}.
Numeric comparisons in these saved results use Kruskal–Wallis because assumptions
fail; full-request independence also fails. Kruskal–Wallis does not repair that
failure. Categorical tests use chi-square, with sparse-table calibration as saved:
${sparse_method}. Vital comparisons exclude missing exposure/target pairs separately;
unknown multi-value responses are excluded from presence/absence comparisons.

The case study proposes an ordinal Kruskal–Wallis test for severity. The implementation
instead tests severity as categorical independence with Cramér's V. This is a valid
association question but does not specifically test an ordered trend and departs
from the proposed severity test. ANOVA/Tukey branches exist for suitable assumptions;
they were not forced onto these results.

### Primary sensitivity results

This table uses the seeded sample with one request per patient, selected before
per-variable missingness exclusions. It addresses repeated observations without
proving independence from other clustering or confounding.

${hypothesis_primary_table}

${primary_significant_n} primary comparisons are significant after Holm correction.
Recorded sex is not significant; this does not establish equivalence or fairness.
The age and any-comorbidity effects are modest relative to severity/red-flag
association. Effect scales differ: Cramér's V is categorical association, while the
saved rank epsilon-squared convention is `${rank_effect_formula}`. Neither
is a clinical risk difference. No practical-effect threshold was prespecified.

### Full-request results and individual items

${hypothesis_full_table}

These full-request p-values are nominal because patients repeat. There are
${changed_conclusions_n} changed adjusted decisions between the full-request and
single-request sensitivity analyses in the saved comparison. Agreement in a single
seeded sample does not establish robust inference across alternative samples.

${hypothesis_secondary_table}

A stored zero p-value denotes floating-point underflow, not zero probability. Sparse
Monte Carlo p-values have finite resolution. Holm adjustment is across the defined
primary-plus-secondary omnibus family within each analysis.

### Post-hoc evidence and practical significance

Significant adjusted omnibus tests gate Dunn comparisons for Kruskal–Wallis and
pairwise category proportions for categorical tests; sparse contrasts use Fisher
exact. Holm is applied within each parent/cohort's post-hoc family. Tukey's already
adjusted p-values receive additional conservative Holm when that branch applies.
Across saved cohorts there are ${posthoc_n} contrasts:

${posthoc_table}

For example, patient-sensitivity oxygen-saturation contrasts have the following
saved median differences in percentage points, first group minus second:

${posthoc_example}

Statistical significance does not identify a safe clinical threshold or quantify
benefit from acting on a prediction. Post-hoc correction is local to each parent,
not global simultaneous control across every selected contrast. Full results and
interpretations are in [post-hoc outputs](@/artifacts/posthoc_results.csv) and
[the sensitivity comparison](@/artifacts/hypothesis_sensitivity_comparison.csv).

## Experimental methodology

Only intake information available at request submission is eligible. Assessment
notes, assessor/assessment identifiers, escalation, protocol and assessment time are
excluded. Request/patient IDs and raw timestamps are metadata for grouping/history,
never learned inputs. Cleaning and fixed vocabularies are deterministic; all learned
imputation, encoding, scaling, resampling and weighting are fit inside pipelines.

Numeric features use median imputation and scaling; ordinal fields preserve order;
categoricals use one-hot encoding; fixed multi-hot and availability indicators pass
through. Class balancing is computed inside each fit. Optional TF-IDF is disabled.
No model was selected using the test outcomes.

### Split and repeated-patient handling

Patients are assigned by their first labelled-request chronological window. Later
requests from an earlier-window patient are purged rather than moved into a later
split. This enforces patient disjointness and strict retained chronology, while
reducing later cohorts and shifting the estimand toward new labelled patients.

${split_table}

Excluded patient counts can overlap retained patient counts and must not be added
as independent patient totals. [Frozen request IDs](@/artifacts/splits.json) and
split tests record the design. All earlier full-cohort EDA and saved hypothesis
analysis had already seen the eventual holdout cohort. Therefore this is a held-out
modelling evaluation, not a fully blinded independent clinical validation.

### Baseline, comparison and tuning

The prespecified balanced logistic baseline was fit on training and evaluated on
validation only: macro F1 ${baseline_f1}, High recall ${baseline_high}, Critical recall
${baseline_critical}. It is a reference experiment, not the final refit.

Latest session ${session_n} contains ${runs_n} logged rows, including
${default_runs_n} default model/imbalance combinations; all preserved sessions contain
${all_runs_n} rows. Algorithm families are Logistic Regression, Decision Tree,
Random Forest, XGBoost and LightGBM. Imbalance comparisons include none, balanced
class weights, random oversampling, random undersampling and SMOTE. Resampling
occurs only during fit. Standard SMOTE interpolates encoded ordinal/binary features
and can produce fractional category indicators; this limits its interpretation.

The table selects the best default imbalance configuration **within each family**
using the existing development rank; it is not a test comparison or an unbiased
estimate after selection.

${default_comparison_table}

Selected candidates use randomized search with ${search_iterations} sampled settings
and ${cv_folds} patient-disjoint forward training folds. The saved CV objective is
`${cv_scoring}`. Default-versus-tuned validation outcomes are:

${tuning_table}

Tuned candidates did not necessarily improve validation F1 or recall. Training CV
and later validation differ in patient/time composition; tuning outcomes should not
be presented as guaranteed gains.

### Calibration, decision rules and stability

Forward-fold training ensembles were calibrated with sigmoid and isotonic methods.
Matching uncalibrated ensembles separate calibration from ensembling/training-size
changes. Class-specific thresholds and cost-weighted decisions were evaluated on
validation with illustrative, unvalidated costs.

${calibration_table}

Reliability curves and bin support are available in
[calibration artifacts](@/artifacts/experiments/calibration/). Brier is the sum of
squared class-probability errors; log loss and Brier are lower-is-better summaries,
not certification of clinical calibration. Changing only a decision rule leaves
probability scores unchanged. Sparse bins and isotonic instability limit conclusions.

The saved stability procedure is: ${bootstrap_description}. These intervals are
conditional on fixed validation predictions; they do not capture refitting/seed
uncertainty or correct for selection. Forward-CV variation is recorded separately.
For the selected configuration, validation patient-bootstrap High/Critical recall
SD is ${selected_bootstrap_sd}; forward-CV High/Critical recall SD is
${selected_cv_sd}. Repeated validation reuse makes shortlist estimates optimistic.

